---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/lc/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/lc/Footer.astro';
import Container from '../../components/lc/Container.astro';
import ArticleHeader from '../../components/lc/ArticleHeader.astro';
import Stats from '../../components/lc/Stats.astro';
import Prose from '../../components/lc/Prose.astro';
import Label from '../../components/lc/Label.astro';
import CtaPanel from '../../components/lc/CtaPanel.astro';
import Button from '../../components/lc/Button.astro';
import Screenshot from '../../components/Screenshot.astro';
import { facts } from '../../data/facts';

// Every number, title, name and date on this page comes from src/data/facts.ts.
// Do not retype one here - npm run check:facts will fail the build.
---

<Layout
  title="Curriculum Intelligence Dashboard"
  description={`A Google Apps Script web app that turned scattered curriculum documents into a live analytics system tracking ${facts.curriculum.courses} courses and ${facts.curriculum.units} units across three school divisions, deployed to ${facts.scale.leaders} leaders at ${facts.aisc.schoolShort} Chennai.`}
>
  <Nav />
  <main>

    <ArticleHeader
      back={{ href: '/work', label: 'Back to all work' }}
      label="Flagship Project · Google Apps Script"
      sublabel="Chapter 4 · Become the institution's memory"
      title="Curriculum Intelligence Dashboard"
    >
      A curriculum analytics web app that made an entire school's curriculum visible, searchable,
      and actionable, and became the first onboarding resource {facts.scale.incomingTeachers} incoming teachers received before
      they ever arrived in Chennai.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <Container>
      <article class="flex max-w-3xl flex-col gap-lc-8 pt-lc-8">
        <Stats
          items={[
          { value: facts.curriculum.courses, label: 'Courses tracked' },
          { value: facts.curriculum.units, label: `Units · ${facts.curriculum.completion} complete` },
          { value: facts.scale.leaders, label: 'Leaders using it' },
          { value: '3', label: 'School divisions' },
          ]}
        />

        <Prose>

          <Label>Overview</Label>
          <h2>What it is</h2>
            <p>
              The Curriculum Intelligence Dashboard is a Google Apps Script web application that gives
              an entire IB World School a single, living view of its curriculum across Elementary, Middle,
              and High School. Instead of curriculum living in hundreds of disconnected documents, leaders
              get a navigable dashboard: a course Navigator, a Standards view with a Visible-for-Learning
              heatmap and gap analysis, a Head-of-Department view, a Gantt-style curriculum Calendar, and
              an AI tab for deeper queries.
            </p>
            <p>
              Underneath it sits the {facts.aisc.schoolShort} Curriculum Documentation Tracker, a structured system tracking
              <strong>{facts.curriculum.courses} courses and {facts.curriculum.units} units</strong> across all three divisions,
              reaching <strong>{facts.curriculum.completion} school-wide completion</strong> by June 2026.
            </p>

          <Label>The Problem</Label>
          <h2>Curriculum nobody could see</h2>
            <p>
              Like most schools, {facts.aisc.schoolShort}'s curriculum knowledge was real but invisible. It lived in
              individual planning documents, in teachers' heads, and in formats that varied by division
              and department. Leaders couldn't answer basic strategic questions, which standards are
              under-assessed, where are the gaps, how complete is our documentation, without weeks of
              manual auditing.
            </p>
            <p>
              When documentation isn't visible, it doesn't get used, it doesn't stay consistent, and it
              walks out the door every time a teacher leaves. The school needed institutional knowledge
              to become an asset rather than a liability.
            </p>

        <Screenshot
          src="/images/work/curriculum-dashboard.png"
          alt="The Curriculum Intelligence Dashboard, showing completion by division and course"
          caption={`The view ${facts.scale.leaders} leaders opened: completion rates per division, drillable to the individual unit.`}
        />

          <Label>What I Built</Label>
          <h2>A live analytics layer over curriculum</h2>
            <p>
              I designed and built the whole system in Google Apps Script so it lived natively inside the
              school's existing Google Workspace, no new platform, no new login, no new vendor. The web
              app reads from the documentation tracker and surfaces it through purpose-built views:
            </p>
          <ul>
            {[
              ['Navigator', 'Browse and search every course and unit across ES, MS, and HS from one place.'],
              ['Standards view', 'A Visible-for-Learning heatmap plus gap analysis that shows which standards are covered, over-covered, or missing.'],
              ['HoD view', 'A department-level lens so Heads of Department can see and steward their own area.'],
              ['Curriculum Calendar', 'A Gantt-style timeline of when units are taught across the year.'],
              ['AI tab', 'Natural-language querying over the curriculum data for deeper, ad-hoc analysis.'],
            ].map(([title, desc]) => (
              <li><strong>{title}.</strong> {desc}</li>
            ))}
          </ul>
          <p>
            The system was in real, active use months before any formal launch, a curriculum coach was
            already flagging live bugs in August 2025, well ahead of the June 2026 rollout to the full
            leadership team.
          </p>

        <Screenshot
          src="/images/work/curriculum-tracker.png"
          alt="The underlying Curriculum Documentation Tracker spreadsheet"
          caption={`The tracker underneath the dashboard, ${facts.curriculum.courses} courses and ${facts.curriculum.units} units in one structure.`}
        />

          <Label>Impact &amp; Results</Label>
          <h2>From a project to onboarding infrastructure</h2>
          <Stats
            items={[
              { value: facts.scale.leaders, label: 'Leaders deployed to', note: '10 SLT + 40+ ILT members' },
              { value: facts.curriculum.completion, label: 'School-wide completion', note: `${facts.curriculum.units} units documented` },
              { value: facts.scale.incomingTeachers, label: 'New teachers onboarded', note: 'before arriving in Chennai' },
            ]}
          />
            <p>
              The dashboard was deployed to 10 Senior Leadership Team members and 40+ Instructional
              Leadership Team members. But the clearest signal of impact came from how the underlying
              Shared Drive curriculum structure got used: in May 2026, the school director sent it to
              <strong>26 incoming 2026–27 teachers</strong> as the very first
              onboarding resource they received, before they had even arrived.
            </p>
          <blockquote>
            "This is phenomenal work throughout this year that has provided extensive insight into our program."
            <footer>{facts.people.galaty.name}, {facts.people.galaty.title}</footer>
          </blockquote>
          <blockquote>
            "Over the past year, we have been working to move all of our curriculum documentation into a
            shared Google Drive structure to create greater consistency, accessibility, and alignment
            across the school."
            <footer>{facts.people.bevans.name}, Director, in the onboarding email sent to {facts.scale.incomingTeachers} incoming teachers</footer>
          </blockquote>

          <Label>Lessons Learned</Label>
          <h2>What I'd carry forward</h2>
          <ul>
            {[
              ['Build where people already are.', 'Living inside Google Workspace, no new platform, removed every adoption barrier. The best tool is one nobody has to be convinced to log into.'],
              ['Ship before you launch.', 'Having a curriculum coach catch real bugs in August, ten months before the formal rollout, meant the June launch landed on something already battle-tested.'],
              ['Make knowledge visible and it becomes infrastructure.', 'The moment curriculum became searchable and complete, it stopped being a documentation chore and became the thing a director hands new hires on day zero.'],
            ].map(([title, desc]) => (
              <li><strong>{title}</strong> {desc}</li>
            ))}
          </ul>

        </Prose>
      </article>
    </Container>

    <CtaPanel title="Curious what a curriculum dashboard could look like for your school?">
      <Fragment slot="actions">
        <Button href="/work-with-me#contact">Let's Talk</Button>
        <Button href="/work" variant="secondary" tone="dark">More Case Studies</Button>
      </Fragment>
    </CtaPanel>

  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>
